13	/**
14	 * Holds each thread at the first row lock it asks for until an expected number of threads
15	 * have asked, so that a test can arrange for several transactions to be holding one lock
16	 * each at the moment any of them reaches for a second.
17	 *
18	 * <p><b>Why a test needs this at all.</b> A latch that releases two threads at the start of
19	 * an operation lines up their <em>beginnings</em>, and nothing else. Against an in-memory
20	 * database the whole of a reservation — two locks, an update and an insert — runs in well
21	 * under the time it takes to schedule the second thread, so the first transaction routinely
22	 * commits before the second acquires anything, and a design that took its locks in an order
23	 * that can deadlock passes anyway. That is the shape of green test the reservation tickets
24	 * were warned about: it exercises the code twice rather than concurrently.
25	 *
26	 * <p>The latest point a test can reach is the statement on its way to the driver, which
27	 * Hibernate offers through {@link StatementInspector}, and a {@code select … for update} is a
28	 * lock acquisition about to happen. Holding threads there puts all of them inside their
29	 * transactions, at their first lock, in the same instant — which is as close to the
30	 * acquisition as anything outside the engine gets.
31	 *
32	 * <p><b>What that is not.</b> {@code inspect} runs <em>before</em> the statement executes, so
33	 * the barrier cannot guarantee that one thread had acquired its first lock before another
34	 * asked for its second: released together, a thread may still run its whole transaction
35	 * while the other is between the barrier and its own first row. What the barrier removes is
36	 * the systematic case — a thread that had not started when the other committed — and what
37	 * takes the argument the rest of the way is the falsification table in
38	 * {@code docs/design-decisions/13-reserve-funds.md}: with the barrier in place, two separate
39	 * defects injected into {@code AccountLocking} and {@code AccountRepository} each turn a test
40	 * red, repeatably. Waiting at the <em>second</em> lock instead would be a stronger guarantee
41	 * and a broken one — under the correct ascending-ID design the second thread is blocked in
42	 * the driver on its first row and never reaches a second statement to be counted.
43	 *
44	 * <p>It is <em>only</em> a test hook: nothing in {@code src/main} knows this exists, and it
45	 * is inert until {@link #holdEachThreadAtItsFirstRowLock} arms it. That is what makes it a
46	 * fair trade where a hook compiled into the production path would not have been.
47	 *
48	 * <p>Register it with {@code @Import(RowLockBarrier.class)} on the test class that needs it.
49	 * Doing so gives that class an application context of its own rather than the one
50	 * {@link BootedApplicationTest} shares, which costs one extra boot — the price of a
51	 * concurrency claim that can fail.
52	 */
