10	import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;
11	import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThatThrownBy;
12	
13	/**
14	 * What {@link CheckLedger#record} does with an answer that has already been given, and with
15	 * one nobody asked for.
16	 *
17	 * <p>The guard is the whole point of the method: a Check service delivers at least once, so
18	 * the same Verdict arrives twice and a contradicting one can arrive after it. Both have to
19	 * leave the ledger saying what it said the first time — the update is guarded on the row
20	 * still being unanswered, so the second delivery matches no row, and what the caller gets
21	 * back is the decision the ledger already supported rather than a refusal.
22	 *
23	 * <p>No database and no Spring: the claims here are about what the method does with the two
24	 * answers the repository can give it, and a fake ledger says which of the two it got. That
25	 * the guarded update really is guarded in SQL is
26	 * {@code RecordedVerdictsAdvanceTheTransferTest}'s, and it has to be — a fake that guards
27	 * where the real statement does not would agree with itself.
28	 */
29	class RecordingAVerdictAnswersOneOutstandingCheckTest {
30	
